    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "Beyond that, horizontal scaling enters the picture. A Lightsail Load Balancer in front of two or more identically configured instances removes the single point of failure that a lone instance represents, and health checks let the load balancer stop routing traffic to an instance that's failing without manual intervention. Teams running containerized workloads can skip instance management altogether and use Lightsail Container Service, which handles image deployment, scaling within defined limits, and public endpoint exposure, functioning as a lightweight alternative to running your own ECS cluster."
    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "The most advanced pattern - and the one that marks the practical ceiling of Lightsail - is using it as the \"simple compute\" layer of a hybrid architecture, connected via VPC peering to a broader AWS account that hosts things Lightsail doesn't offer natively: Lambda for event-driven processing, SQS or SNS for messaging, CloudWatch for deeper observability, or Aurora for database workloads that need more scale than Lightsail's managed database tier provides. At this point, many teams find that the operational overhead of straddling two paradigms exceeds the simplicity they were originally buying, and that's the natural signal to migrate fully into EC2 and its surrounding services - which Lightsail's AMI export feature makes possible without rewriting the application."
    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.h2, {
